WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.066
Hello Insiders,
I'm Rene Ritchie, your Creator Liaison

00:00:03.133 --> 00:00:04.968
and today I'm talking to Thomas Kim.

00:00:05.035 --> 00:00:06.570
TK, what do you do at YouTube?

00:00:06.637 --> 00:00:08.038
Hey Rene, thanks for having me on.

00:00:08.105 --> 00:00:09.873
I am a Product Management Director

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:12.142
I particularly oversee creator monetization.

00:00:12.209 --> 00:00:14.311
So, as a part of that role,
I do a couple of things.

00:00:14.378 --> 00:00:15.846
I run the YouTube Partner Program.

00:00:15.913 --> 00:00:19.383
I do a lot of work to make sure that
we have a good amount of ad revenue

00:00:19.449 --> 00:00:20.817
and the third thing I do is

00:00:20.884 --> 00:00:23.654
I make sure that
we have a healthy, vibrant creator ecosystem.

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:25.556
So along those lines,
we're getting a lot of questions

00:00:25.622 --> 00:00:27.157
from creators about reused content.

00:00:27.224 --> 00:00:28.325
Can you tell us what that is?

00:00:28.392 --> 00:00:29.826
One of the really important things

00:00:29.893 --> 00:00:31.962
we're trying to do
with the YouTube Partner Program

00:00:32.029 --> 00:00:35.799
is that we reward creators that
are making really novel, original content.

00:00:35.899 --> 00:00:38.702
And so reused content is when

00:00:38.769 --> 00:00:41.205
there's a video that features footage

00:00:41.271 --> 00:00:42.506
maybe from a TV clip

00:00:42.573 --> 00:00:45.209
or it's another creator's videos

00:00:45.776 --> 00:00:48.745
and it's uploaded without
a lot of modification to it, right?

00:00:48.812 --> 00:00:50.614
So what we're really
trying to prevent there

00:00:50.747 --> 00:00:52.482
is a scenario where

00:00:52.583 --> 00:00:56.386
someone is profiting off of content
that was generated by somebody else

00:00:56.453 --> 00:00:58.355
without adding a lot of value.

00:00:58.455 --> 00:01:01.692
And why do we care particularly
about reused content?

00:01:01.859 --> 00:01:04.828
It's both I think for the platform
and the creator, right?

00:01:04.895 --> 00:01:07.764
So, for the platform,
when users come to YouTube

00:01:07.831 --> 00:01:10.467
they want to see new, unique things
that they can't find elsewhere.

00:01:10.534 --> 00:01:11.835
And so when they come to YouTube

00:01:11.902 --> 00:01:14.571
and there's just a lot of
the same content that's reused

00:01:14.638 --> 00:01:16.773
it really makes YouTube
less valuable for the user.

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:19.309
But also, from a creator standpoint,
this is also really important.

00:01:19.409 --> 00:01:22.412
At the end of the day,
the YouTube Partner Program is designed to

00:01:22.546 --> 00:01:25.048
reward the creators
for all the hard work they put in

00:01:25.115 --> 00:01:27.017
all the creativity
and originality they put in.

00:01:27.084 --> 00:01:30.187
You can imagine a scenario where
if someone else just literally took that clip

00:01:30.254 --> 00:01:32.689
reuploaded it with very little work on that

00:01:32.756 --> 00:01:35.425
and profited off that, that doesn't set
the right incentives for creators

00:01:35.492 --> 00:01:38.729
and that's why it's really important for
the YouTube Partner Program to prevent that.

00:01:38.795 --> 00:01:42.599
So, how can creators avoid being
either rejected or suspended from YPP

00:01:42.666 --> 00:01:43.800
for reused content?

00:01:43.867 --> 00:01:46.537
So there are a couple of things,
so I think at a simple level

00:01:46.603 --> 00:01:49.640
just do something
that's very original and unique, right?

00:01:49.706 --> 00:01:52.476
That would be the best way
to prevent this from happening.

00:01:52.543 --> 00:01:54.211
Now, there are other things you could do.

00:01:54.278 --> 00:01:59.316
So, one, if you are using clips
or footage from other sources

00:01:59.383 --> 00:02:01.618
a TV show, a clip, anime, whatever

00:02:01.685 --> 00:02:03.887
just make sure you're putting
your original spin on it

00:02:03.954 --> 00:02:07.090
that could take the form of
explaining what's going on, add comments

00:02:07.491 --> 00:02:09.426
put a really interesting angle on that

00:02:09.626 --> 00:02:11.361
splice it with your own content.

00:02:11.461 --> 00:02:15.465
So, anything that really makes that content
and makes it novel in a different way

00:02:15.532 --> 00:02:18.235
can reinterpret it,
is what we're looking for you to do.

00:02:18.302 --> 00:02:20.370
The other thing is that,
tell us a little bit about

00:02:20.437 --> 00:02:22.940
how you went about this process,
how did you conceive the idea

00:02:23.006 --> 00:02:24.608
how did you make the content?

00:02:24.675 --> 00:02:26.844
That will also help us
understand what you're doing

00:02:26.910 --> 00:02:28.812
and simply avoids the risk that

00:02:28.912 --> 00:02:30.647
this is a video made by someone

00:02:30.714 --> 00:02:33.717
who is just trying to upload
other people's things and make money off it.

00:02:33.784 --> 00:02:37.654
And do we look at the video level
or the channel level for reused content?

00:02:37.721 --> 00:02:39.990
Most things we do
in the YouTube Partner Program

00:02:40.057 --> 00:02:41.959
we're really evaluating
the channel as a whole

00:02:42.025 --> 00:02:45.495
we want to make sure we are comfortable
being a partner with this channel

00:02:45.562 --> 00:02:47.497
and so a lot of this is all at a channel level.

00:02:47.598 --> 00:02:51.502
It's not just the one video,
you should look at your entire back catalog

00:02:51.635 --> 00:02:54.905
to make sure that all the videos there
are consistent with this policy.

00:02:54.972 --> 00:02:57.508
And what about if I,
as a creator, have multiple channels?

00:02:57.574 --> 00:03:00.944
Like maybe I have a dubbed language version
or I have a podcast channel

00:03:01.078 --> 00:03:02.813
and then a clips channel.

00:03:02.946 --> 00:03:05.883
Should I put something
in the metadata or the descriptions

00:03:05.949 --> 00:03:07.918
that help YouTube understand
they are connected?

00:03:07.985 --> 00:03:09.253
Yeah that would really help us.

00:03:09.319 --> 00:03:12.155
For example, taking a bunch of
your podcasts and slicing clips

00:03:12.222 --> 00:03:13.357
it'd be helpful if you said

00:03:13.423 --> 00:03:15.459
"Hey, this is a compilation of my clips."

00:03:15.526 --> 00:03:18.462
We have a lot of creators that
are taking potentially their short content

00:03:18.529 --> 00:03:20.063
and making compilations, that's fine

00:03:20.130 --> 00:03:22.533
but just let us know,
"Hey, these are my shorts

00:03:22.599 --> 00:03:24.067
that I've made into a compilation."

00:03:24.168 --> 00:03:26.870
Those things really help
when we evaluate a channel.

00:03:26.937 --> 00:03:29.006
And what about,
when in doubt, if I'm not certain

00:03:29.072 --> 00:03:31.775
should I be trying to explain
how I make the videos

00:03:31.842 --> 00:03:33.310
what are the best practices?

00:03:33.377 --> 00:03:35.112
Yeah, you know again, a couple of things.

00:03:35.179 --> 00:03:37.481
We want to see something
that's original, that's yours

00:03:37.548 --> 00:03:40.350
Rene Ritchie has a way
to express things into the world

00:03:40.417 --> 00:03:42.419
so I think you should
look at the content and say

00:03:42.486 --> 00:03:44.888
"Hey, is this truly unique,
is this something only I can do?"

00:03:44.955 --> 00:03:49.293
It reflects your genuine personality
or your character

00:03:49.359 --> 00:03:52.129
and again, to the extent
that you are using other footage

00:03:52.196 --> 00:03:56.300
make sure it's something that you're using
but you're adding your own spin to it

00:03:56.366 --> 00:03:57.267
that's really unique.

00:03:57.367 --> 00:03:59.236
And so, whether that's you reacting to it

00:03:59.303 --> 00:04:01.305
making a comment
about it, adding to it...

00:04:01.371 --> 00:04:02.239
Sharing my expertise.

00:04:02.306 --> 00:04:04.274
Sharing your expertise
is what we're looking for.

00:04:04.341 --> 00:04:06.610
There are a lot of things
I think we can do better there

00:04:06.677 --> 00:04:08.579
my team is working on
a couple of experiences

00:04:08.645 --> 00:04:11.014
that we'd like to roll out
to make this easier for creators

00:04:11.114 --> 00:04:13.250
but in the meantime,
when in doubt, just make sure

00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:16.286
you're making content that is
somewhat unique and original to you.

00:04:16.353 --> 00:04:17.721
Any feedback you'd like to get?

00:04:17.788 --> 00:04:18.856
Yeah I'd love to get that.

00:04:18.922 --> 00:04:20.624
Like I said,
we're working on a few things

00:04:20.691 --> 00:04:23.360
so if creators have feedback
or suggestions, we'd love to hear it.

00:04:23.427 --> 00:04:26.230
Insiders, you know what to do,
leave everything in the comments below.

00:04:26.296 --> 00:04:27.931
Thank you for watching and keep it real.

